    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-90536",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required",
      "problem": "$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978511/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
